Wide bandgap (WBG) semiconductors use materials with wider energy bandgaps than conventional silicon, enabling efficient switching under demanding voltage, temperature, and frequency conditions. Silicon carbide (SiC) is increasingly used in high-voltage power conversion, while gallium nitride (GaN) supports compact, high-frequency power electronics and selected radio-frequency applications. The market includes commercial WBG semiconductor devices and modules rather than the full value of vehicles, inverters, charging systems, or telecommunications equipment incorporating them.

What Is the WBG Semiconductor Market?
The WBG semiconductor market covers the commercial sale of semiconductor devices and modules manufactured using silicon carbide, gallium nitride, and other qualifying wide bandgap materials. Products include power transistors, diodes, power modules, and RF and high-frequency devices.

These components are used in electric mobility, charging infrastructure, renewable energy systems, industrial power conversion, data centers, and communications. The market excludes conventional silicon-only semiconductors, upstream substrate sales counted separately, and the revenue of complete electrical systems.

Key Market Drivers
Electric Mobility and Charging Infrastructure
Electric vehicle traction inverters and charging systems require power devices capable of managing high voltages and switching losses. SiC MOSFETs and diodes offer performance advantages in selected high-voltage applications, supporting their adoption in automotive power electronics. Manufacturers are investing in production capacity and device qualification to serve growing electrification requirements.

Industrial and Renewable Energy Conversion
Renewable energy systems, battery storage, motor drives, and industrial converters increasingly evaluate WBG devices to improve power-conversion efficiency and thermal performance. SiC modules can serve high-power applications, while GaN devices support compact switching designs. Growing investment in energy infrastructure creates opportunities for qualified component suppliers.

Advanced GaN Power Electronics
GaN transistors offer fast switching and high power density in suitable applications, including compact power supplies and data center equipment. Continued product development and volume production are helping expand the range of commercial designs in which GaN can compete with conventional silicon devices.

