Market Challenges
WBG device manufacturers must manage complex fabrication processes, substrate quality, packaging, and reliability requirements. SiC production depends on defect control, wafer quality, and manufacturing yields, while GaN devices require careful attention to packaging, gate driving, and electromagnetic compatibility. Automotive and industrial customers also require extensive electrical, thermal, and lifetime validation before adopting new components in volume production.

Market Restraints
High Substrate and Manufacturing Costs
SiC substrate production and device fabrication involve demanding process requirements. Scaling toward 200 mm manufacturing can improve long-term production economics, but the transition requires capital investment, process development, and yield improvement. These factors can constrain cost reductions and supply availability.

Long Qualification and Reliability Cycles
Automotive and industrial applications require rigorous testing before a new semiconductor device can enter sustained production. Qualification delays can slow commercial adoption even when a product offers improved electrical performance.

Competition from Conventional Silicon
Silicon devices remain cost-effective in many applications. Where the efficiency or thermal advantages of SiC and GaN do not justify their additional component and system costs, equipment designers may continue selecting established silicon technologies.

